com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/25eff71610bb5c90d817d7fad3cb58449e2aaf3b/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00"}]},{"sha":"e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGU3OWE5NzVmYzRkMTE5MjJlOTJmZGJlNGJkYmU0OTM2YjRkZWNjMDA","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-22T15:06:29Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-22T15:06:29Z"},"message":"ci: gate the rename programme's rule 7 instead of trusting review (#863)\n\nWave 0 of the sunset programme — the prerequisite the plan implies but\ndoes not schedule.\n\n## Why this is first\n\nHard rule 4 says the do-not-touch list becomes CI. **It is not CI.** The\nonly occurrences of the old brand in `.github/workflows` today are the\nPostgres role and database — which are sub-wave 3b's own target — plus\ncomments. So rule 7, *mint nothing new carrying the old brand*, is\ncurrently enforced by whoever happens to be reviewing.\n\nThe programme runs for weeks with ordinary feature work alongside it.\nEvery old-brand name that lands meanwhile adds a redirect somebody keeps\nforever and spends a rename option already paid for. The gate is a grep;\nwhat it prevents is doing Wave 5 twice.\n\n## What it does\n\n`scripts/legacy_name_ratchet.py` counts the lines carrying the old brand\n**per file**, in the tree being built and in `main`, and fails on any\nfile that went up. Decreases are the point of the programme, so they're\nreported as progress:\n\n```\nNo new lines. 47 removed by this change (-47 net).\n```\n\nTwo design calls worth stating, because both were the difference between\na gate that survives the programme and one that gets switched off:\n\n- **Per file, not per repo total.** A total hides an addition behind an\nunrelated deletion in the same PR — during a programme whose whole shape\nis \"totals go down\", which is exactly when nobody looks. A per-file\ncount also keeps a within-file move at net zero, which is most of what\nwaves 3–5 do; a per-line diff would flag every move.\n- **Against `main`'s tree, not a committed baseline.** A baseline goes\nstale, churns on every move, and has to be regenerated by the same\nperson the gate is pointed at. On a `pull_request` the checkout is\nalready the merge commit, so comparing against `main` *is* this PR's\ncontribution, and there is nothing to maintain. Same anonymous\n`--depth=1` fetch the broker-contract gate already uses.\n\n## The escape hatch, which rule 3 requires\n\nOld names stay readable forever, so legitimate additions exist — a\ncompat alias, a redirect, a test pinning the old wire format. A line\ncarrying `legacy-name-ok` is exempt:\n\n```python\nLEGACY_TOOL_ALIAS = \"...\"  # legacy-name-ok: permanent shim, plugins pinned to v1\n```\n\nIt exempts **only its own line** — otherwise one marker at the top of a\nfile would silence the file — so every exception lands in the diff with\nits reason, where a reviewer can disagree. `CONTRIBUTING.md` documents\nthe rule, the marker and the two commands.\n\n## The hole it found in itself\n\n`git grep` exits 1 for \"no matches\" **and** for a pathspec that matches\nnothing, both with an empty stderr. So a broken pattern or pathspec\nreads as a clean tree, and the gate passes every PR from then on without\nchecking anything — a silent no-op, the worst failure available to a\ngate.\n\n`main()` now refuses to pass when *both* trees come back empty: that is\neither a broken scan or a finished migration, and both want a human.\n\nA first draft carried a `returncode == 1 and not stderr` guard that\nlooked like it handled this. Measuring the actual exit codes showed a\nbad ref exits 128, so the clause was unreachable and the fail-closed\ntest was passing for the wrong reason. Removed, and replaced with a\nguard for the case that is real.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_legacy_name_ratchet.py`, 13 tests, each building a throwaway\ngit repo and running the real script. Mocking `git grep` would test the\narithmetic and none of the part that breaks — output parsing, the\ntree-ish prefix and case-insensitivity are all git's behaviour.\n\nEvery design was simulated against the finished tree; in each case\nexactly the intended tests failed:\n\n| simulated design | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| exempt marker not honoured | both marker tests |\n| marker exempts the whole file | `..._exempts_only_its_own_line` |\n| repo total instead of per file | 4, incl.\n`..._not_masked_by_a_deletion_elsewhere` |\n| split one field too far | 9 |\n| pathspec matches nothing | 10 |\n| no both-empty guard |\n`..._finding_nothing_at_all_fails_rather_than_passing` |\n\nThe split-field case is the one worth keeping: it corrupts the path into\na fragment of the matched line, so the real file's count never rises and\nthe gate passes while appearing to work.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **The gate failed this PR twice while I wrote it** — first on the\nscript's own pattern constant, then on prose in my comments that\nmentioned the brand. Both fixed by rewording rather than exempting,\nwhich is the behaviour it should encourage. It passes itself now.\n- The branch was originally named with the old brand. Renamed, for the\nsame reason.\n- Current footprint here: **2,463 lines across 280 files**. `--report`\nprints the breakdown.\n- **This repo only.** Replicating to the other repos is a follow-up, one\nat a time, per rule 6.\n\n## Gates\n\n`ruff check` clean on both new files. `actionlint` reports one\nshellcheck finding at `ci.yml:87`, which is pre-existing — it reproduces\non `origin/main` — and none in the added job. Full suite **4785 passed,\n0 failed**. Rebased onto `bdb20a24` after #861 landed; gate and tests\nre-verified on the new base.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"9d9d0988fbc0a9be040e625b60259f40c69823f3","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/9d9d0988fbc0a9be040e625b60259f40c69823f3"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqibr1CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4W4QABA6ewDRd7ng8Nse5+Jc2/zI\nOP7bETelnx49tORY2KrXcTUrburtCPDeJ2hVWRSMlUInY+4ipX0nw20ZQLLZliYO\nmQD7+eKLWth7c85J+P0PL2bmxZkNVwQWPGwrVbHDcA65Tb7IfnLVvr8V/f9Ar0Qd\nywA78JRMHuTSj1nwqKOT8s703qFqJ97KQwOGsyEFUgcIADrt5W3gZSLXui1cGkw1\njrdT9nZcPuhLvzbFJR/aUUb9Rmvq2U4dumMdxez0V5ILS0DFK3sPHEj2KOEYYFRo\nZQyH6sddEicu3Sf8JA9oz94SgFntTD0NhYjJ/GlUMdD0m3rbdwfzFK9LNXoVhZka\nUQToESuMV7hsttqVGM8Rd4bIyx6Hx79ygoANMVvZFgw8DyyHQigzmtbV97fxZbKa\nKgpk6B3r45/vf3E3o/yJ+Q/0M/tQg1D76b1bJe7MLqL8DNhFb2T1vZeIdXmaSwgY\nXTkRI8cwVYt4o6ynKawJ5mFbG/sU1J7ibjW6LtFyZ4bh1W+VpYcUAaoPciknmQpo\nuJlBMB4nlDm1K1TlqE8rJInX3BHILQQ9oB8MDZ3bH7hCpKtPQ7ZvU+XIXUY3zqR9\nkMU1TR0UNHughuGw2Ktm0k5s8gnrG19sBivUgycjInRxx3wZHFN0Fa/xltZV70y3\ndBg6EZ8QR3tuSc6fDQjf\n=TetL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 9d9d0988fbc0a9be040e625b60259f40c69823f3\nparent 433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1787411189 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787411189 +0300\n\nci: gate the rename programme's rule 7 instead of trusting review (#863)\n\nWave 0 of the sunset programme — the prerequisite the plan implies but\ndoes not schedule.\n\n## Why this is first\n\nHard rule 4 says the do-not-touch list becomes CI. **It is not CI.** The\nonly occurrences of the old brand in `.github/workflows` today are the\nPostgres role and database — which are sub-wave 3b's own target — plus\ncomments. So rule 7, *mint nothing new carrying the old brand*, is\ncurrently enforced by whoever happens to be reviewing.\n\nThe programme runs for weeks with ordinary feature work alongside it.\nEvery old-brand name that lands meanwhile adds a redirect somebody keeps\nforever and spends a rename option already paid for. The gate is a grep;\nwhat it prevents is doing Wave 5 twice.\n\n## What it does\n\n`scripts/legacy_name_ratchet.py` counts the lines carrying the old brand\n**per file**, in the tree being built and in `main`, and fails on any\nfile that went up. Decreases are the point of the programme, so they're\nreported as progress:\n\n```\nNo new lines. 47 removed by this change (-47 net).\n```\n\nTwo design calls worth stating, because both were the difference between\na gate that survives the programme and one that gets switched off:\n\n- **Per file, not per repo total.** A total hides an addition behind an\nunrelated deletion in the same PR — during a programme whose whole shape\nis \"totals go down\", which is exactly when nobody looks. A per-file\ncount also keeps a within-file move at net zero, which is most of what\nwaves 3–5 do; a per-line diff would flag every move.\n- **Against `main`'s tree, not a committed baseline.** A baseline goes\nstale, churns on every move, and has to be regenerated by the same\nperson the gate is pointed at. On a `pull_request` the checkout is\nalready the merge commit, so comparing against `main` *is* this PR's\ncontribution, and there is nothing to maintain. Same anonymous\n`--depth=1` fetch the broker-contract gate already uses.\n\n## The escape hatch, which rule 3 requires\n\nOld names stay readable forever, so legitimate additions exist — a\ncompat alias, a redirect, a test pinning the old wire format. A line\ncarrying `legacy-name-ok` is exempt:\n\n```python\nLEGACY_TOOL_ALIAS = \"...\"  # legacy-name-ok: permanent shim, plugins pinned to v1\n```\n\nIt exempts **only its own line** — otherwise one marker at the top of a\nfile would silence the file — so every exception lands in the diff with\nits reason, where a reviewer can disagree. `CONTRIBUTING.md` documents\nthe rule, the marker and the two commands.\n\n## The hole it found in itself\n\n`git grep` exits 1 for \"no matches\" **and** for a pathspec that matches\nnothing, both with an empty stderr. So a broken pattern or pathspec\nreads as a clean tree, and the gate passes every PR from then on without\nchecking anything — a silent no-op, the worst failure available to a\ngate.\n\n`main()` now refuses to pass when *both* trees come back empty: that is\neither a broken scan or a finished migration, and both want a human.\n\nA first draft carried a `returncode == 1 and not stderr` guard that\nlooked like it handled this. Measuring the actual exit codes showed a\nbad ref exits 128, so the clause was unreachable and the fail-closed\ntest was passing for the wrong reason. Removed, and replaced with a\nguard for the case that is real.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_legacy_name_ratchet.py`, 13 tests, each building a throwaway\ngit repo and running the real script. Mocking `git grep` would test the\narithmetic and none of the part that breaks — output parsing, the\ntree-ish prefix and case-insensitivity are all git's behaviour.\n\nEvery design was simulated against the finished tree; in each case\nexactly the intended tests failed:\n\n| simulated design | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| exempt marker not honoured | both marker tests |\n| marker exempts the whole file | `..._exempts_only_its_own_line` |\n| repo total instead of per file | 4, incl.\n`..._not_masked_by_a_deletion_elsewhere` |\n| split one field too far | 9 |\n| pathspec matches nothing | 10 |\n| no both-empty guard |\n`..._finding_nothing_at_all_fails_rather_than_passing` |\n\nThe split-field case is the one worth keeping: it corrupts the path into\na fragment of the matched line, so the real file's count never rises and\nthe gate passes while appearing to work.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **The gate failed this PR twice while I wrote it** — first on the\nscript's own pattern constant, then on prose in my comments that\nmentioned the brand. Both fixed by rewording rather than exempting,\nwhich is the behaviour it should encourage. It passes itself now.\n- The branch was originally named with the old brand. Renamed, for the\nsame reason.\n- Current footprint here: **2,463 lines across 280 files**. `--report`\nprints the breakdown.\n- **This repo only.** Replicating to the other repos is a follow-up, one\nat a time, per rule 6.\n\n## Gates\n\n`ruff check` clean on both new files. `actionlint` reports one\nshellcheck finding at `ci.yml:87`, which is pre-existing — it reproduces\non `origin/main` — and none in the added job. Full suite **4785 passed,\n0 failed**. Rebased onto `bdb20a24` after #861 landed; gate and tests\nre-verified on the new base.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-22T15:06:30Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/e79a975fc4d11922e92fdbe4bdbe4936b4decc00/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56"}]},{"sha":"433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKDQzM2QxNWMxYTVjMWZhZmNlNzY3MDY2MmZlZjIwYWQ3ODM4MTViNTY","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-22T14:38:54Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-22T14:38:54Z"},"message":"test(interview): mint tenants the end-of-run sweep can actually see (#866)\n\nFixes #858.\n\n## Not a race — accumulated state, and my original diagnosis was wrong\n\nI filed #858 as a job-claim race on the shared `synthesis_sem`, inferred\nfrom the code because it would not reproduce. It reproduced today —\n**deterministically, and in isolation**, which the report explicitly\nsaid it did not do. That difference is the answer.\n\n`tests/conftest.py` cleans up with `DELETE FROM {table} WHERE tenant_id\nLIKE 'test-tenant-%'`, and `documents` is in its table list. The two\ninterview files minted tenants as `get_test_auth(f\"t-{uid()}\")` — a\nprefix that `LIKE` does not match. So every interview job either file\nhad ever written was still there:\n\n```\ninterview_jobs   done              286\n                 failed_permanent   12\n                 processing          2   <- reclaimed by the sweep on every run\n\ntenants with interview jobs:    228\nmatched by the conftest sweep:    0\n```\n\nThe endpoint under test is **cross-tenant**:\n`process_pending_interview_jobs` loops over every tenant taking pending\nand stale-`processing` jobs. Those two stuck rows qualified on every\nrun, from tenants no test knew about — so `jobs_processed >= 1` passed\non residue while `jobs_done`, which counts only rows reaching\n`committed`, stayed 0. That is exactly the `1 processed / 0 done` shape\nobserved.\n\nCI provisions a fresh Postgres per run, which is why CI stayed green\nwhile a local database degraded from \"1 in 3\" to \"every time\" over a day\nof running the suite.\n\n## The fix\n\n34 call sites now use a new `conftest.new_tenant_id()`, and the prefix\nmoves into `SWEEP_TENANT_PREFIX` — used by every minter (`TENANT_ID`,\nthe `tenant_id` fixture, `new_tenant_id`) and by the `DELETE`s, which\nare parameterised on it instead of repeating the literal.\n\nThe prefix was load-bearing while documented as a convention in a\ndocstring. This sweep is the only thing that ever removes rows written\nthrough the service layer, because most tests write via a storage client\nthat commits on its own connection where the per-test rollback never\nreaches. One constant is what makes the two sides agree by construction\nrather than by everyone remembering.\n\n## Verified by what stops accumulating\n\nNot \"the test is green\" — it was green in isolation this morning too:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3 consecutive runs of the two tests | fails | **3× pass** |\n| `interview_jobs` + `interview_watermarks` left behind | ~100 per run,\nforever | **0** |\n| full interview suites (47 tests) | — | pass, leaving 0 rows |\n| full suite | 1 failed | **4815 passed, 0 failed** |\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_tenant_sweep_coupling.py`, 4 tests pinning the coupling\nrather than any call site. Each confirmed to fail against the way it can\nbreak:\n\n| simulated break | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| a minter hardcodes its own prefix | the minter + fixture tests |\n| a `DELETE` hardcodes the pattern | the no-literal + binding tests |\n| a table added to the sweep with an inline pattern |\n`..._binds_the_prefix` |\n| the fixture diverges from the plain function |\n`..._fixture_mints_a_sweepable_id` |\n\nTwo notes worth keeping:\n\n- The no-literal test **parses rather than greps**, so only code counts.\nDocstrings and comments here quote the prefix while explaining it, which\nis the opposite of a problem — written as a grep first, it failed on\nthis change's own documentation.\n- The third case is the realistic regression: a table appended to the\nsweep list with its own inline pattern would leak silently while every\nother table stayed clean.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe interview files only. The same mismatch affects at least\n`test_reports.py`, `test_security_tenant_isolation.py` and others —\n**12,714 rows across seven tables were unswept on the measured database,\nof which 0 matched**. Filed as #865 with the numbers and three options;\nconverting the rest, or making the sweep prefix-agnostic, is a decision\nabout the fixture rather than a flake fix and wants taking as one\nchange.\n\nThis PR's guard protects the conftest's internal coupling. It\ndeliberately does not guard call sites — such a check would be red\ntoday, for the reason in #865.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","tree":{"sha":"fd096ea21f7fa90d8841993f178ee872d20c1465","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/fd096ea21f7fa90d8841993f178ee872d20c1465"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqibR+CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAilkQAK5dL+pMntfVKcWzyw4EVRPF\nBkDOF7e3Cv6ltWKXPltBAW6xj1Uhdsa5z0qQxGPb7bwdC7Xff2nK+uKzBQ5xvgm8\ntWiMUHT7Lj6KqKx5odMqsunsqAGo2yDEM3fPsWipfd7hO+UwgddIZQDJY1S1yPEw\nRpqeucC4yRU4m7Fn6H0duiHUZsZzmTGTzYB47NSxHHlawAlhjCefDu9Fb31rf9sp\nFt1gVW6vFfPceuthL6ujdoueqoDWgVhsjb7drZtL4s1ECr7NTDT5abjXruBoomhT\nkZ3pZAIl62Faonr0TF4dyK+FnSuLyhPOmT+rXow6l9EgqZRgUDG5eVoUdxC/cxRg\nv0zD0bdwoFatjUsUyXK0C/d8TGHwxzaLBgJreMEYG2YSMc+f3/S4w13EMCDdd0B/\n4s78JIiNlTNHY/uChc4JI24YndFLxfPHEd118LIBCMKPiOzPZdLGFtD3irMbjC+7\nL0Wmfgv1f+xVExcMVASAM67APisckI+8DyPc0R0knxdVX4ahPHKdEX7BsnHwF91r\npwEpp6WS7W+aTTEkX1QszLQKyO7NPvNk03sG6XI5CR8qvX4hM6O8lZwmA6jdQNIH\n399TGaPOS7vMKOSARdpqNqxB9o8Gof91Q9VH1G62sE/ByKQkfZ8+rdWQ0tnp5817\nM3xcttYceH5gZNqgUsqV\n=gZ0K\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree fd096ea21f7fa90d8841993f178ee872d20c1465\nparent c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1787409534 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787409534 +0300\n\ntest(interview): mint tenants the end-of-run sweep can actually see (#866)\n\nFixes #858.\n\n## Not a race — accumulated state, and my original diagnosis was wrong\n\nI filed #858 as a job-claim race on the shared `synthesis_sem`, inferred\nfrom the code because it would not reproduce. It reproduced today —\n**deterministically, and in isolation**, which the report explicitly\nsaid it did not do. That difference is the answer.\n\n`tests/conftest.py` cleans up with `DELETE FROM {table} WHERE tenant_id\nLIKE 'test-tenant-%'`, and `documents` is in its table list. The two\ninterview files minted tenants as `get_test_auth(f\"t-{uid()}\")` — a\nprefix that `LIKE` does not match. So every interview job either file\nhad ever written was still there:\n\n```\ninterview_jobs   done              286\n                 failed_permanent   12\n                 processing          2   <- reclaimed by the sweep on every run\n\ntenants with interview jobs:    228\nmatched by the conftest sweep:    0\n```\n\nThe endpoint under test is **cross-tenant**:\n`process_pending_interview_jobs` loops over every tenant taking pending\nand stale-`processing` jobs. Those two stuck rows qualified on every\nrun, from tenants no test knew about — so `jobs_processed >= 1` passed\non residue while `jobs_done`, which counts only rows reaching\n`committed`, stayed 0. That is exactly the `1 processed / 0 done` shape\nobserved.\n\nCI provisions a fresh Postgres per run, which is why CI stayed green\nwhile a local database degraded from \"1 in 3\" to \"every time\" over a day\nof running the suite.\n\n## The fix\n\n34 call sites now use a new `conftest.new_tenant_id()`, and the prefix\nmoves into `SWEEP_TENANT_PREFIX` — used by every minter (`TENANT_ID`,\nthe `tenant_id` fixture, `new_tenant_id`) and by the `DELETE`s, which\nare parameterised on it instead of repeating the literal.\n\nThe prefix was load-bearing while documented as a convention in a\ndocstring. This sweep is the only thing that ever removes rows written\nthrough the service layer, because most tests write via a storage client\nthat commits on its own connection where the per-test rollback never\nreaches. One constant is what makes the two sides agree by construction\nrather than by everyone remembering.\n\n## Verified by what stops accumulating\n\nNot \"the test is green\" — it was green in isolation this morning too:\n\n| | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3 consecutive runs of the two tests | fails | **3× pass** |\n| `interview_jobs` + `interview_watermarks` left behind | ~100 per run,\nforever | **0** |\n| full interview suites (47 tests) | — | pass, leaving 0 rows |\n| full suite | 1 failed | **4815 passed, 0 failed** |\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_tenant_sweep_coupling.py`, 4 tests pinning the coupling\nrather than any call site. Each confirmed to fail against the way it can\nbreak:\n\n| simulated break | caught by |\n|---|---|\n| a minter hardcodes its own prefix | the minter + fixture tests |\n| a `DELETE` hardcodes the pattern | the no-literal + binding tests |\n| a table added to the sweep with an inline pattern |\n`..._binds_the_prefix` |\n| the fixture diverges from the plain function |\n`..._fixture_mints_a_sweepable_id` |\n\nTwo notes worth keeping:\n\n- The no-literal test **parses rather than greps**, so only code counts.\nDocstrings and comments here quote the prefix while explaining it, which\nis the opposite of a problem — written as a grep first, it failed on\nthis change's own documentation.\n- The third case is the realistic regression: a table appended to the\nsweep list with its own inline pattern would leak silently while every\nother table stayed clean.\n\n## Scope\n\nThe interview files only. The same mismatch affects at least\n`test_reports.py`, `test_security_tenant_isolation.py` and others —\n**12,714 rows across seven tables were unswept on the measured database,\nof which 0 matched**. Filed as #865 with the numbers and three options;\nconverting the rest, or making the sweep prefix-agnostic, is a decision\nabout the fixture rather than a flake fix and wants taking as one\nchange.\n\nThis PR's guard protects the conftest's internal coupling. It\ndeliberately does not guard call sites — such a check would be red\ntoday, for the reason in #865.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>","verified_at":"2026-08-22T14:38:55Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/433d15c1a5c1fafce7670662fef20ad783815b56/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c"}]},{"sha":"c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGMxNTliZjFkNThmMWJhYjU2NGMzNTVlODNmOWE0NWNkZDdiYzZlN2M","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-22T12:30:31Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-22T12:30:31Z"},"message":"docs(llm): say why the Vertex and Gemini SDK imports are deferred, and enforce it (#864)\n\nI set out to hoist `vertex.py`'s function-level imports to module scope,\nwhich is what the top-level-imports rule asks for. **That would have\nbeen a\nregression**, so this documents and enforces the exception instead.\n\n## Why hoisting breaks\n\nThe deferral is about optional dependencies, not circular imports:\n\n* `google-cloud-aiplatform` is an **extra** for core-worker\n  (`vertex = [...]` in its `pyproject.toml`), and only a hard dependency\n  for core-api.\n* `google-genai` appears **zero times** in core-worker's\n`pyproject.toml` — not required, not optional. core-worker never has it.\n\nVerified rather than assumed, by blocking `vertexai` and\n`google.cloud.aiplatform` on the import path:\n\n```\nA. import common.llm.providers.vertex  ->  OK (module loads without the SDK)\n   class present: True\nB. top-level SDK import -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'vertexai'\n   => hoisting makes vertex.py unimportable in a no-extra install\n```\n\nDeferred, an install without Vertex fails only if it actually CALLS\nVertex\n— which is what an optional provider should do. Hoisted, the module\ncannot\nbe imported at all, and `common/llm/_platform.py` imports it inside a\n`try`\non the strength of exactly that property.\n\n## Why it read as an oversight\n\n`gemini.py:45` already explains its own lazy import — and cites this\nfile\nas the precedent: *\"same pattern as VertexLLMProvider with vertexai\"*.\nSo\nthe file everything else points at was the one copy that never said why.\nThat is the actual defect, and it is why I flagged it as a rule\nviolation\nin the first place.\n\n`registry.py` also deliberately omits `vertex` from the top-level\nprovider\nimports it does for fake/gemini/openai. Those three placements are one\ndecision; changing any alone breaks it, which the module docstring now\nsays.\n\n## A comment cannot stop the next hoist\n\n`tests/test_optional_provider_sdks_stay_optional.py` pins the invariant\nfor\nboth providers: with the SDK hidden behind a meta-path finder, each\nmodule\nmust still import and still expose its provider class.\n\nWithout it the rule argues for the change and nothing argues back until\nsomething fails at deploy time in the one install that lacks the\npackage.\nConfirmed to bite: hoisting the import makes\n`test_module_imports_without_its_sdk[vertex-without-aiplatform]` fail\nwith\nthe simulated `ModuleNotFoundError`.\n\nEach case carries a control asserting the blocker really does hide the\npackage — otherwise the guard would pass no matter where the imports\nlived.\nRaising from `find_spec` rather than falling through is what makes it\nabsence and not a redirect; falling through would resolve from\nsite-packages, where these packages do exist in the dev environment.\n\n## Verification\n\n`4811 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed` — full suite, zero failures. All 12\nCI\nruff scopes clean. No behaviour change: comments, a docstring, and a new\ntest.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6b1e5d3762cd686faa18dfafe70458826af90819","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/6b1e5d3762cd686faa18dfafe70458826af90819"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqiZZnCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAArwUQALEW8fy3RvjfU32vEQ3P/Yi4\nqYAFnOk0TD5DF48rTNB/tHwhWg8oz6QUi7nT9ZIcNFLC4MvHgf7BegmeluZLTc5u\nzlGcW3xsYcvyw/6XvkDd75lkktWmYiDlFnjilsdhfWxAutETfmBXOK8KutTG93HX\nKMKv2NcHmigZfeaj26PRXs8y2FxQIiKb4TCBaUUONLxmJlvrSmhSCCuIyLM7mYl6\n6AjeoBtL7KFgdBH1A8he7ZE3XYT0mEyRwIbaM72F/KgLf7iqOwUwSMNKMumarseJ\nwLm1pSP09ToL0ys3ZF/MzfywDxi4op5DXwLwOfI5aSxpUTITnBWcCGDZxIyI0tTa\noqRVnU1hjqF1eRbVXsqV5QBvwepjmpN1afKXdyO/MxkF0zqP8DIBCRS/ioDCj1cs\n0va3E6pTL2wTrHEoGjxZMJi4GZ1sA7jnP9F/JPDS1DJwxVw8vfv82j6pWR+xb7ei\n5RPkJpuIOwVAVY1PbGSy3WoY5igCsj/OfsQk6X7jtZ8qCc6+rN5IyXwkSNO0xTEC\nYtk8AIKx9yCpPtBYRtvd5EoR/vG/HlOdIgL/GnBHRziJQMciANrUF6/Cdl9b6Fzt\nQK9DD5xtbW2cyPwea34+UbQQItpKb2YPWDKwl7OgH5c0zpg0gbSvm3FGHezwRnzb\nBxi2cu1T9bCEQL/wdHi2\n=C7OP\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","payload":"tree 6b1e5d3762cd686faa18dfafe70458826af90819\nparent b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88\nauthor Eldad Dahan <eldad@caura.ai> 1787401831 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1787401831 +0300\n\ndocs(llm): say why the Vertex and Gemini SDK imports are deferred, and enforce it (#864)\n\nI set out to hoist `vertex.py`'s function-level imports to module scope,\nwhich is what the top-level-imports rule asks for. **That would have\nbeen a\nregression**, so this documents and enforces the exception instead.\n\n## Why hoisting breaks\n\nThe deferral is about optional dependencies, not circular imports:\n\n* `google-cloud-aiplatform` is an **extra** for core-worker\n  (`vertex = [...]` in its `pyproject.toml`), and only a hard dependency\n  for core-api.\n* `google-genai` appears **zero times** in core-worker's\n`pyproject.toml` — not required, not optional. core-worker never has it.\n\nVerified rather than assumed, by blocking `vertexai` and\n`google.cloud.aiplatform` on the import path:\n\n```\nA. import common.llm.providers.vertex  ->  OK (module loads without the SDK)\n   class present: True\nB. top-level SDK import -> ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'vertexai'\n   => hoisting makes vertex.py unimportable in a no-extra install\n```\n\nDeferred, an install without Vertex fails only if it actually CALLS\nVertex\n— which is what an optional provider should do. Hoisted, the module\ncannot\nbe imported at all, and `common/llm/_platform.py` imports it inside a\n`try`\non the strength of exactly that property.\n\n## Why it read as an oversight\n\n`gemini.py:45` already explains its own lazy import — and cites this\nfile\nas the precedent: *\"same pattern as VertexLLMProvider with vertexai\"*.\nSo\nthe file everything else points at was the one copy that never said why.\nThat is the actual defect, and it is why I flagged it as a rule\nviolation\nin the first place.\n\n`registry.py` also deliberately omits `vertex` from the top-level\nprovider\nimports it does for fake/gemini/openai. Those three placements are one\ndecision; changing any alone breaks it, which the module docstring now\nsays.\n\n## A comment cannot stop the next hoist\n\n`tests/test_optional_provider_sdks_stay_optional.py` pins the invariant\nfor\nboth providers: with the SDK hidden behind a meta-path finder, each\nmodule\nmust still import and still expose its provider class.\n\nWithout it the rule argues for the change and nothing argues back until\nsomething fails at deploy time in the one install that lacks the\npackage.\nConfirmed to bite: hoisting the import makes\n`test_module_imports_without_its_sdk[vertex-without-aiplatform]` fail\nwith\nthe simulated `ModuleNotFoundError`.\n\nEach case carries a control asserting the blocker really does hide the\npackage — otherwise the guard would pass no matter where the imports\nlived.\nRaising from `find_spec` rather than falling through is what makes it\nabsence and not a redirect; falling through would resolve from\nsite-packages, where these packages do exist in the dev environment.\n\n## Verification\n\n`4811 passed, 3 skipped, 1 xfailed` — full suite, zero failures. All 12\nCI\nruff scopes clean. No behaviour change: comments, a docstring, and a new\ntest.\n\nSigned-off-by: eldad-caura <eldad@caura.ai>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","verified_at":"2026-08-22T12:30:32Z"}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/c159bf1d58f1bab564c355e83f9a45cdd7bc6e7c/comments","author":{"login":"Eldad-Caura","id":250841599,"node_id":"U_kgDODvOJ_w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/250841599?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura","html_url":"https://github.com/Eldad-Caura","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Eldad-Caura/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"web-flow","id":19864447,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/commits/b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88","html_url":"https://github.com/caura-ai/caura/commit/b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88"}]},{"sha":"b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88","node_id":"C_kwDOSOAAX9oAKGIxODRjMjgwNDkxMzc1NDk0YzI0ZGY5OTVmMDdjZGMxNzkyZWViODg","commit":{"author":{"name":"Eldad Dahan","email":"eldad@caura.ai","date":"2026-08-22T06:00:43Z"},"committer":{"name":"GitHub","email":"noreply@github.com","date":"2026-08-22T06:00:43Z"},"message":"feat(llm): give the retry loop a deadline it can actually enforce (#862)","tree":{"sha":"6fd751a24b93f3a9f3861854a078f4126428cb99","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/trees/6fd751a24b93f3a9f3861854a078f4126428cb99"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/caura-ai/caura/git/commits/b184c280491375494c24df995f07cdc1792eeb88","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":true,"reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqiTsLCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA62kQADbRFu9QVGwmDnyV1QiJ/x11\nlQc8PvROkZOWMrXDhewyRs16it8u/wxJ0PFQJoO08J2iC7pFBXmqV8N3EOrXlwwE\nGRQNEPcm6hz9Oi2Fn5L0bOpZNkVD3T/NHHBMAGA3uKzwzmI+aAOGnU421GsBANpk\nD/ZTlnFCstMlA2Lq1DrZKO6dPeGEYW6KZuaPeGnni0PXdt1s+FqiRyw5q01t3GMo\nDKVNVqitKGdfvIhV/NDdGpDyqB70FaBPFXxseMGLxDQQirHbGv/53l0vxntbDZ0q\nCHin2NCPqhrequM3jC+nwAbNHJnapvKkRmEO9wCm2QmKdvFum8NNa7KH/1RPzN+4\n+4x4aLvs3cyanlcEum4POsUHCOyLYuNRO6xJefzU13Tpi61ZZvG9qnhXnlpolDo/\nksY/a6BvM64KHrzkKS3D5ACGGAA7GEpSMkzkx6gkfg+4T+lM8y8wgm9Ye2klPFuz\nML65JXgkLES5KtJ+m1c+rN8/VLOWGiEA0eHhk77gJlx/A4w+X/aTQg5vy+aAhDNU\nbfavyT5OyPxb48+TRxJSAsiWzmWwUNnZZLUiRhxsY